  and validator; a [38;5;153mstorage backend[39m with a 3-method interface ([38;5;153mread[39m/[38;5;153mwrite[39m/[38;5;153mremove[39m) whose only implementation today is
  [38;5;153mlocalStorageBackend[39m; and a [38;5;153mpreferences[39m store that composes them and exposes typed [38;5;153mget(key)[39m / [38;5;153mset(key, value)[39m /
  [38;5;153mreset()[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m the seam is one 3-method object, so a future server backend is a new file, not a rewrite. Defaults and
  validation live in one declarative place, so adding a third pref is one schema entry, not four new functions. Tests
  inject an in-memory backend — no DOM, no [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m shim. [3mCosts:[0m three files instead of one; a little indirection
  for two prefs. [3mRules out:[0m nothing. [1mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. B plus a versioned, migrating payload[0m — everything in B, and the stored JSON carries [38;5;153m{version, data}[39m with a
  migration chain run on read.
  [3mBuys:[0m renaming or retyping a pref later can't strand users on old data. [3mCosts:[0m real machinery — migration functions,
  version constants, tests for each hop — to protect two prefs whose shape you control and that cost nothing to discard.
  [3mRules out:[0m nothing. [1mComplexity: high.[0m

  [1mI recommend B.[0m It's the approach that actually delivers the seam you picked, and the indirection pays for itself
  immediately in testability — with the backend injectable, the whole store is testable in plain Node with no browser.
  I'd skip C's versioning: YAGNI. B already degrades safely on unrecognized stored data by falling back to defaults,
  which is the same user-visible outcome a migration would produce for prefs this simple, at a fraction of the cost. If
  the pref set grows into something users would be upset to lose, versioning is a clean addition on top of B later.

  On the two rung-1 consequences I flagged: under B, [38;5;153mrememberedUsername[39m is opt-in-shaped — I'll have it written only
  when the login succeeds, and I'll add a [38;5;153mreset()[39m so it can be cleared. Still plaintext [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m; that property
  doesn't go away.
